{"reference_id": "1", "content": "Class: RaycastHit2D\nDescription: Returns information about an object detected by a raycast in 2D physics. A raycast is used to detect objects that lie along the path of a ray and is conceptually like firing a laser beam into the Scene and observing which objects are hit by it. The RaycastHit2D class is used by Physics2D.Raycast and other functions to return information about the objects detected by raycasts. Additional resources: Physics2D.Raycast, Ray2D class.\nProperty centroid: The centroid of the primitive used to perform the cast.\nProperty collider: The collider hit by the ray.\nProperty distance: The distance from the ray origin to the impact point.\nProperty fraction: Fraction of the distance along the ray that the hit occurred.\nProperty normal: The normal vector of the surface hit by the ray.\nProperty point: The point in world space where the ray hit the collider's surface.\nProperty rigidbody: The Rigidbody2D attached to the object that was hit.\nProperty transform: The Transform of the object that was hit."}
{"reference_id": "2", "content": "Class: Ray\nDescription: Representation of rays. A ray is an infinite line starting at origin and going in some direction.\nProperty direction: The direction of the ray.\nProperty origin: The origin point of the ray.\nMethod GetPoint: Returns a point at distance units along the ray.\nMethod ToString: Returns a formatted string for this ray.\nConstructor Ray: Creates a ray starting at origin along direction."}
{"reference_id": "3", "content": "Class: PhysicsScene\nDescription: Represents a single instance of a 3D physics Scene.\nMethod BoxCast: Casts the box along a ray and returns detailed information on what was hit.\nMethod CapsuleCast: Casts a capsule against all colliders in this physics scene and returns detailed\ninformation on what was hit.\nMethod InterpolateBodies: Interpolates Rigidbodies in this PhysicsScene.\nMethod IsEmpty: Gets whether the physics Scene is empty or not.\nMethod IsValid: Gets whether the physics Scene is valid or not.\nMethod OverlapBox: Find all colliders touching or inside of the given box, and store them into the buffer.\nMethod OverlapCapsule: Check the given capsule against the physics world and return all overlapping\ncolliders in the user-provided buffer.\nMethod OverlapSphere: Computes and stores colliders touching or inside the sphere into the provided\nbuffer.\nMethod Raycast: Casts a ray, from point origin, in direction direction, of length maxDistance,\nagainst all colliders in the Scene.\nMethod ResetInterpolationPoses: Resets the Transform positions of interpolated and extrapolated Rigidbodies in\nthis PhysicsScene to Rigidbody.position and Transform rotations to\nRigidbody.rotation.\nMethod Simulate: Simulate physics associated with this PhysicsScene.\nMethod SphereCast: Casts a sphere along a ray and returns detailed information on what was hit."}
{"reference_id": "4", "content": "Class: Screen\nDescription: Provides access to display information. Use this class to get a list of supported screen resolutions, change the current resolution, or hide/show the system mouse pointer. When you launch your application, Unity immediately needs to use settings like the screen's resolution and full- screen mode to render the first frame before any custom code starts to run. This means Unity doesn't initially use your code to configure these settings. Instead, Unity gets the values of these settings from command line arguments, Player settings, or from PlayerPrefs. Priority of initial startup settings Unity determines the initial startup settings based on the following order of priority (highest to lowest): 1. Command line arguments: If you specify a setting's value via command line, Unity prioritizes and uses that setting. 2. Changed Player settings: If you don't use the command line to configure the setting, and you modify the setting's value in the Player settings, Unity uses those values. For example, in the Player settings, if you change Fullscreen Mode, Default Screen Width, or Default Screen Height, Unity will use those values to change the screen's full-screen mode and resolution at startup. 3. Stored PlayerPrefs: If you don't specify a setting's value via command line or Player settings, Unity checks if the setting is stored in PlayerPrefs, and uses the setting if found. 4. Default Player settings: Otherwise, Unity uses the original default Player settings \n... (truncated) ...\nty of the screen measured in dots-per-inch (DPI)\n(Read Only).\nProperty fullScreen: Enables full-screen mode for the application.\nProperty fullScreenMode: Set this property to one of the values in FullScreenMode to change the\ndisplay mode of your application.\nProperty height: The current height of the screen window in pixels (Read Only).\nProperty mainWindowDisplayInfo: The display information associated with the display that the main\napplication window is on.\nProperty mainWindowPosition: The position of the top left corner of the main window relative to the top left\ncorner of the display.\nProperty orientation: Specifies logical orientation of the screen.\nProperty resolutions: Returns all full-screen resolutions that the monitor supports (Read Only).\nProperty safeArea: Returns the safe area of the screen in pixels (Read Only).\nProperty sleepTimeout: A power saving setting, allowing the screen to dim some time after the\nlast active user interaction.\nProperty width: The current width of the screen window in pixels (Read Only).\nMethod GetDisplayLayout: Retrieves layout information about connected displays such as names, resolutions,\nand refresh rates.\nMethod MoveMainWindowTo: Moves the main window to the specified position relative to the top left corner of the\nspecified display. Position value is represented in pixels. Moving the window is an\nasynchronous operation, which can take multiple frames.\nMethod SetResolution: Switches the screen resolution and refresh rate if supported."}
{"reference_id": "5", "content": "Class: RaycastHit\nDescription: Structure used to get information back from a raycast. Additional resources: Physics.Raycast, Physics.Linecast, Physics.RaycastAll.\nProperty articulationBody: The ArticulationBody of the collider that was hit. If the collider is not attached to an\narticulation body then it is null.\nProperty barycentricCoordinate: The barycentric coordinate of the triangle we hit.\nProperty collider: The Collider that was hit.\nProperty colliderInstanceID: Instance ID of the Collider that was hit.\nProperty distance: The distance from the ray's origin to the impact point.\nProperty lightmapCoord: The uv lightmap coordinate at the impact point.\nProperty normal: The normal of the surface the ray hit.\nProperty point: The impact point in world space where the ray hit the collider.\nProperty rigidbody: The Rigidbody of the collider that was hit. If the collider is not attached to a rigidbody\nthen it is null.\nProperty textureCoord: The uv texture coordinate at the collision location.\nProperty textureCoord2: The secondary uv texture coordinate at the impact point.\nProperty transform: The Transform of the rigidbody or collider that was hit.\nProperty triangleIndex: The index of the triangle that was hit."}
